<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pura Luhur Uluwatu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Uluwatu Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pura Luhur Uluwatu is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> sea temple on a clifftop at the south-western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, one of the island's principal directional temples. It is associated with the 16th-century priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dang-hyang-nirartha">Dang Hyang Nirartha</a>, who is said to have attained spiritual liberation there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pura-luhur-uluwatu.jpg" alt="Pura Luhur Uluwatu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paskuu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pura_Luhur_Uluwatu_2017-08-17_(34).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple stands on the south-western tip of the Bukit Peninsula in Pecatu Village, South Kuta, in Badung Regency. It is the only Balinese sea temple, or pura segara, that is also one of the nine directional temples, and it is dedicated to Sang Hyang Widhi Wasa in his manifestation as Rudra. The name joins ulu, edge, and watu, rock: the temple is built at the edge of a cliff 70 metres high that projects into the sea. In folklore the rock is part of the petrified barque of Dewi Danu.</p><p>A Kecak dance based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> is performed on the cliff side every day at 6 pm, against the sunset. Long-tailed macaques live at the temple and snatch visitors' belongings, which they can usually be persuaded to give back for fruit or nuts. Studies of these monkeys suggest that they learn this bartering and pass it on to their young.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A small temple is claimed to have existed on the site earlier, but the structure was significantly expanded by a Javanese sage, Empu Kuturan, in the 11th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dang-hyang-nirartha">Dang Hyang Nirartha</a>, another sage from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, is credited with introducing the padmasana shrines, and it is said that he attained moksha here, an event known locally as ngeluhur, to go up. This gave the temple its epithet Luhur, height.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 2009 a spatial planning regulation, Bali Provincial Regulation No. 16, set a zone of 5 km from the temple's outer wall in which no building was allowed except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> religious facilities, so that both settlements and tourist buildings were outlawed. This conflicted with the customary law, awig-awig, of the Pecatu village community, which protected the temple forest within a radius of 350 m. Applying the state law would have meant demolishing many existing buildings.</p><p>At least seven lawsuits were brought by individuals, groups and customary communities of Pecatu, and the Supreme Court rejected the appeals in 2010 and 2013. In 2015 the provincial government issued Regulation Number 8, which set three types of protection for temples, each with a core, a buffer and a utilisation zone. Uluwatu was classed as type II, with a core zone of up to 1 km, a buffer zone from 1 to 2.5 km and a utilisation zone from 2.5 to 5 km.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uluwatu Temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Javanese priest Dang Hyang Nirartha, who came to Bali in that era, reshaped Balinese Hinduism and founded temples such as Tanah Lot and <b>Uluwatu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gelgel">Kingdom of Gelgel</a></li></ul></section>
